2026-05-13 23:30:39,276 subagents.e_lld_v_agent.agent INFO lld_v[critical] capabilityRecipes.admin-clone-bundle.steps[4] (sql_transaction) — The sql_transaction step contains only one step: the INSERT for the new bundle (`INSERT INTO bundles ... RETURNING id`), binding its result to `newBundle`. The two subsequent item-insert and tier-insert decision steps (steps[5] and steps[6]) are top-level siblings of the sql_transaction, not nested inside it. This means `newBundle[0].id` is referenced inside those sibling steps after the transaction closes — which is a bind-scoping violation (bindResultTo inside sql_transaction.steps is only visible within the transaction). More critically, the item and tier inserts run outside the transaction boundary, so a crash after the bundle row commits but before items/tiers are inserted leaves an orphaned, itemless bundle with no rollback possible.
2026-05-13 23:30:39,276 subagents.e_lld_v_agent.agent INFO lld_v[critical] capabilityRecipes.widget-cart-add.steps (cartCreate mutation variables) — The `cartCreate` mutation variable binding is `"input": "$cartLines"`, where `cartLines` is an array of `{ merchandiseId, quantity }` objects. The Shopify Storefront `CartInput` type expects the lines under a `lines` key (i.e. `{ lines: [...] }`), not a bare array. Passing a raw array as `CartInput` will be rejected by the Storefront API schema, causing every cart-add call to fail with a GraphQL validation error rather than a userError.
2026-05-13 23:30:39,276 subagents.e_lld_v_agent.agent INFO lld_v[important] capabilityRecipes.record-bundle-purchase-on-order-paid — bundle identification logic — The recipe identifies which bundles are present in an order by filtering `discount_codes` for codes prefixed with `BUNDLE_` and parsing `bundleId` out of the code string (e.g. `BUNDLE_{bundleId}_{basisPoints}`). The `bundleId` stored in the DB is a UUID. Shopify discount code strings have a maximum length and may be case-folded or modified in transit; more importantly, the platformGaps entry acknowledges that merchants must manually create matching codes in Shopify Admin. Nothing in the recipe validates that the parsed UUID actually exists in the `bundles` table before attempting the INSERT, meaning a manually mistyped code (wrong UUID segment) causes the FK insert to fail silently via `continueOnError`, recording zero purchase history rows for what was a genuine bundle purchase. The recipe has no fallback to cross-reference line item `variant_id` values against `bundle_items` to identify the bundle independently of the discount code string.
2026-05-13 23:30:39,276 subagents.e_lld_v_agent.agent INFO lld_v[important] capabilityRecipes.handle-inventory-level-update.steps[2] (compute newAvailability) — The expression is `(safePayload.available === null || safePayload.available > 0) ? 'available' : 'out_of_stock'`. When `available` is `null` (the payload field is nullable per `payloadFields`), this maps null to `'available'`, which is semantically wrong: a null available count means the inventory level data is incomplete or the item is not tracked at that location — it should not be treated as in-stock. This causes the recipe to incorrectly transition variants from `out_of_stock` back to `available` on incomplete payloads, which would clear a valid health warning and potentially re-enable a bundle that should remain degraded.
2026-05-13 23:30:39,276 subagents.e_lld_v_agent.agent INFO lld_v[minor] capabilityRecipes.admin-update-bundle.steps[3] (sql_update template) — The UPDATE template uses `COALESCE(${description}, description)` for the description field, but `description` is a nullable column (merchants legitimately want to clear a description by passing `null`). With COALESCE, passing `null` for `description` is indistinguishable from omitting it, so a merchant can never clear an existing description through this route — the old value is always preserved when `null` is supplied.
